Isobar
=======

Isobar is a cross-platform WEFAX (emission J3C) weather-fax decoder that
reimplements the behavior of, and interoperates with, KG-FAX v1.1.3
(K.G, 2009) — including its `.syn` file format, its settings (`.ini`)
schema, and its UI workflow.

Original work
-------------
All source code in this project is original, written from a functional
specification derived from reverse engineering. No code, text, or graphical
assets were copied from the original program. Where the implementation
necessarily converges with the original (e.g., the Hilbert/atan2 FM
demodulation approach, the WEFAX sync-tracking algorithm), that convergence
reflects the small number of natural implementations of a public standard
under the merger doctrine, not copying.

Provenance
----------
WEFAX (Weather Facsimile) is an international transmission standard published
by the World Meteorological Organization: WMO-No. 386, "Manual on the Global
Telecommunication System", Volume I, Part III, section 5 ("Technical
characteristics of equipment for meteorological facsimile (analogue)
transmissions"):
  - FM sub-carrier over radio circuits: centre 1900 Hz, 1500 Hz = black,
    2300 Hz = white (section 5.5.1; also section 5.3.1.2).
  - Index of cooperation 576 or 288 (section 5.1.2).
  - Scanning line frequency 60 / 90 / 120 / 240 rpm (section 5.1.5).
  - IOC-selection tone 300 Hz for IOC 576, 675 Hz for IOC 288
    (section 5.2.2.1); stop signal 450 Hz (section 5.2.5.1).
  - Phasing signal, 30 s at 1.0 / 1.5 / 2.0 / 4.0 Hz for 60 / 90 / 120 /
    240 rpm (section 5.2.3.1).

Receiver conformance for this standard is specified by ISO 9876:2015, "Ships
and marine technology — Marine facsimile receivers for meteorological charts",
which covers receivers for charts transmitted per WMO-No. 386, Part III-5.

HF weather-fax broadcasts carry this sub-carrier over single-sideband and are
listed with emission designator J3C (see NOAA, "Worldwide Marine
Radiofacsimile Broadcast Schedules").

These standards are free for anyone to implement.

KG-FAX is freeware, © K.G, 2009. It is NOT open source. The Isobar project
is independent and NOT affiliated with, endorsed by, or derived from KG-FAX.

Interoperability
----------------
Isobar reads and writes KG-FAX `.syn` files for interoperability only. The
`.syn` format, including its radix-255 line-count encoding, is reproduced
exactly because file-format interoperability requires it.

Isobar keeps its own settings in `isobar.ini` and never writes `kgfax.ini`.
It reads an existing `kgfax.ini` once, to carry a user's preferences over
on first run; that reader reproduces the original's section and key names
(including their original spellings) only so far as reading such a file
requires.

